A||||6||loafer87gt||Free engine rebuild! Whee!||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||05-30-2000||02:05 AM||||hey guys, I got some great news. One of my buddies is finishing off his auto mechanics apprenticeship course and to complete it he needs to pull an engine from a car and rebuild the engine. I totally trust the guy so this is great news. It seems like my engine has lost a lot of its original jam but I haven't been able to rebuild it because I have been going to school as well. Now my question is, what sort of components would I need to build my engine so I could safely rev it up RELIABLY to 7000 RPM? I would like to end up with an engine that is more or less bulletproof, like my bro's Rice rocket engine that he takes up to 8000RPM every single friggin shift. Is this possible with the 2.8 or am I dreaming? What sort of stuff would I need to achieve this? Also, has anyone managed to custom fabricate an intake manifold so that we can use the third gen aluminum heads on the 2.8? Any ideas would be appreciated||||reg||1
Z||000001||N_Man||05-30-2000||02:54 AM||||If I had the option it would look somthing likle:<BR>Forged crankshaft<BR>forged pistons<BR>lightweight valves<BR>shotpeened rods<BR>big cam w/strong springs<BR>extensive headwork<P>The list could go on but my biggest problem with revving this engine high is it just doesn't get enough air.  the intake size is pretty small to flow for the high revs.  <P>My dad does most of his work on older 2L 4cyl Alfa Romeo's and he gets them to rev 7500 reliably but they also have 2 webber carbs feeding them, each has an air intake about the size of ours at the throttle body. <P>However if you changed the intake...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||batboy||05-30-2000||04:13 PM||||This is just my opinion and you may choose to ignore it if you want. Reliable and 7,000 rpm do not go together. Inexpensive and 7,000 rpm do not go together either. If you're using flat hydraulic lifters, even with high performance double valve springs, you'll be at the point of floating valves at that rpm. When you float a valve, that's very bad on the valve springs, not to mention you lose power. Once you float a valve and the spring has lost tension, it will begin floating at a lower rpm the next time and even lower yet the time after that, etc...<P>The next thing to address is breathing (flow) capacity. The heads just flat out will not flow worth a crap at those rpm without porting and other expensive work. Headers are a must. For a cam to work good at high rpm, you need lots of duration and lift, which kills your low end torque. Also, a big cam won't work with the ECM, so you'll have to use a carb and change the manifold.<P>You're better off building a low to mid-range high torque engine with a 6 grand redline and just be happy blowing your ricer friend away off the line or in the curves. Just don't get into long straight-line racing where he can get into his upper rpm range for a long period of time. With the Batmobile, I blow them away and then shut it down. The longer you're street racing, the more dangerous it gets and the greater the chance of being pulled over by the police.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-30-2000||04:46 PM||||You dead-on BB. <P>Don't attempt to create a 7000 RPM pushrod 660 engine. You will be sorely and financially disappointed. Unless you plan on road racing where the RPM's are always in the 5000+ range, you want torque. Street racing takes torque.<P>Either go to a larger displacement engine for torque or swap in a 3.4L DOHC for hi RPM. The costs will be the same in the end.<P>IMHO||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||05-30-2000||09:37 PM||||The 2.8 in your car stock only pulls to about 5000 RPM and then the engine runs out of steam. You could put a cam in like the Crane Compucam with springs, ports the bowls and gasket match the intake manifolds. You should also port out the exhaust manifolds and with just those mods you can get your 2.8 to pull to 6000 RPM with only minimal loss of low-end torque. The problem with reving your 2.8 to 7000 RPM is not that the engine cant reliably do it. It is because the induction system (Tuned Port injection) is designed for low end torque and it will not support the kind of airflow that you need to make power at 7000 RPM.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ezsurf||05-30-2000||10:43 PM||||The ECM has a rev limiter at 6000 rpm.  You'll never be able to get it past 6000 revs if you keep it hooked to the ECM.  Plus, you're past peak HP at 5000 rpm.  You're not doing yourself any good reving it higher.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DJRice||05-31-2000||09:22 AM||||Your best bet will probably be to get a decent set of pistons and a new crank...and have your friend port the heads (and exh manifolds to match) so you can get the maximum flow.  Thats probably where youll get the most bang for your buck.<P>||||reg||1
